  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Max brings producer Iosif Prigozhin to the hotel to confirm the car accident to Vika, but Prigozhin is wearing a prop moustache from a music video shoot. Convinced Max hired an impersonator, Vika yanks at his moustache until Prigozhin storms out.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "У вас новый имидж? Усы?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Снимаюсь в новом клипе.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Вика, вот Иосиф.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Здравствуйте.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "vika",
      "t": "Молодец, похож! Поздравляю.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "В смысле?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "vika",
      "t": "А усы не догадались брить?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "vika",
      "t": "Тогда бы совсем похож был.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "vika",
      "t": "Мужчина, идите домой.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "vika",
      "t": "Ваш спектакль затянулся.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Я действительно Иосиф Пригожин!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Вика, подожди! Не отпущу, пока не поверишь.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through their conversation line by line and unpack the Russian phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "У вас новый имидж? Усы?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Do you have a new look? A moustache?' The grammar packs 'at' and 'moustache' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange. the guest answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Снимаюсь в новом клипе.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I am filming in a new music video.' the guest brings out the contrast between 'film' and 'new' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Вика, вот Иосиф.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Vika, here is Iosif.' Max brings out the contrast between 'Vika' and 'here' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Здравствуйте.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Hello.' the guest anchors the line on 'hello', while 'hello' completes the thought. Vika answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "vika",
      "t": "Молодец, похож! Поздравляю.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Well done, he looks like him! Congratulations.' The grammar packs 'well done' and 'congratulations' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "В смысле?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What do you mean?' The grammar packs 'sense' and 'sense' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "vika",
      "t": "А усы не догадались брить?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And did you not think to shave the moustache?' The plural past ending '-li' on the verb for 'guessed' covers a group or the polite 'you'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "vika",
      "t": "Тогда бы совсем похож был.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Then he would have looked completely alike.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'then' before landing on 'was'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "vika",
      "t": "Мужчина, идите домой.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Mister, go home.' Notice how Russian expresses 'man' and 'home' with crisp inflection rather than helper words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "vika",
      "t": "Ваш спектакль затянулся.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Your performance has dragged on.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'your' before landing on 'dragged on'. the guest answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Я действительно Иосиф Пригожин!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I really am Iosif Prigozhin!' the guest brings out the contrast between 'I' and 'really' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Вика, подожди! Не отпущу, пока не поверишь.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Vika, wait! I will not let you go until you believe me.' In Russian, the idea of 'Vika' flows naturally into 'will believe' without extra filler words. Alright, let's hear the entire conversation one more time from start to finish.",
      "gap": 0.9
